  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nIt is a relatively quiet morning, though we are seeing our first \nsolid amount of cloud cover in several days. Temperatures are \nstarting off warm for the day, with widespread 50s and 60s early\nthis morning.\n\nMultiple chances for rain are on the way, stemming from an area\nof low pressure that will deepen along the lee of the Rockies \nlater today. Our first look at showers today will initiate from\nthe increasing WAA, especially across western and central \nMinnesota where southerly winds are expected to gust between 25 \nand 35 mph today. Isolated showers move in as early as 7-9AM \nover southwest Minnesota, and become more scattered along a \nnortheastwared-moving boundary by the afternoon. Forecast \nsoundings reveal a couple hundred J of CAPE across central \nMinnesota, potentially supporting isolated thunderstorms \nembedded within the area of showers.\n\nWarm temperatures today and tomorrow are the last hurrah of this \nlate October \"heatwave,\" as a cold front begins to sweep through\nthe Upper Midwest. The front, and the series of upper waves \ntied to it, will promote continued chances for light rain \nthrough much of the weekend. By that, I do not mean a washout by\nany mean, in fact it is probably the opposite of that. It will \nnot rain the whole weekend, nor will it rain everywhere. In the\nspots that do see rainfall, the amounts will be light Saturday \nand Sunday, primarily at or below 0.1\".\n\nTemperatures crash down to near-normal starting Sunday, and\nanother low pressure travels northeast from the Central \nPlains. This puts us on the northern side of the low, which \noften can mean a greater chance for wintry precipitation, \nthough current NBM temperatures Monday morning range from the \nupper 30s to low 40s. Long range guidance suggest any chance for\nsnowflakes will stay to the north and east of us with this \nsystem. Rainfall amounts still look promising, with a gradual \naccumulation of between 0.5 to 0.75 inches across western \nMinnesota and about 0.2 to 0.5 inches for areas to the east. \nPoPs drop off by Tuesday, but remain between 10 to 30 percent \nthrough much of the rest of the week.\n\nHalloween is starting to get into the range of our forecast, and \ncurrent guidance shows no major sign of a large system that\nweekend, which would keep things roughly around normal \nconditions for late October.",
